testing/fixtures/mountd — what kdos-mountd would offer, on a machine with
nothing plugged in

`sys/` is a /sys/block tree and `dev/` holds hand-built superblocks: a FAT32
one labelled KDOSSTICK on a REMOVABLE disk, and an ext4 one on an internal disk
that must never be offered. `fstab` is an /etc/fstab that claims the stick by
label.

Two disks are boot media. `sdd` carries an ISO 9660 partition and a FAT ESP.
`sde` is the shipped isohybrid image as a stick holds it: `dev/sde` is the
WHOLE disk with the ISO 9660 descriptor at 0x8000, `sde1` is a 64-sector stub
with no filesystem, and `sde2` is the FAT ESP. No partition of `sde` probes as
ISO 9660, so only a rule that probes the disk node finds it. `sdf` is a
whole-disk ext4 stick, the shape of a KDOS_PERSIST store; the selftest mounts
it at /mnt/persist by writing its own mounts file, because a mount line has to
name the fixture's node path.

`mounts-live` is the /proc/mounts of a booted live medium, and it carries one
cifs line: a network share is listed from there and from nowhere else, so a
fixture with no such line could not exercise `shares` or `disconnect` at all.

    kdos-mountd --fixture testing/fixtures/mountd/sys testing/fixtures/mountd/dev

Three assertions come out of it, and each is a refusal that matters more than
the acceptance: the stick is eligible; it stops being eligible the moment an
fstab entry claims it (somebody already decided what that device is for); and
the internal disk is never eligible at all whatever it is formatted with.

Nothing is mounted by `--fixture` and nothing needs root — which is the only
reason selection logic this consequential gets tested at all. The same seam
`kdos stutter --fixture`, `kdos-oomd --fixture` and `KDOS_PRIVACY_PROC` use.
